add('1:a',['A3.2.2'],'A museum has many departments. Each curator and each technician belongs to one department. A curator can work with several technicians, and a technician can work with several curators. Construct an ERD showing these three relationships and their cardinalities.','DEPARTMENT 1—many CURATOR; DEPARTMENT 1—many TECHNICIAN; CURATOR many—many TECHNICIAN. A link entity may resolve the last relationship.',[(1,'Department to curator is one-to-many.'),(1,'Department to technician is one-to-many.'),(1,'Curator to technician is many-to-many, or correct link-entity resolution.')],[4])
add('1:b',['A3.1.1'],'Explain two benefits of replacing separate paper equipment logs with a shared relational database used by several museum departments. Develop each benefit in this setting.','Concurrency control lets staff record loans simultaneously while preventing conflicting updates, so separate departments see consistent equipment availability. Queries can retrieve overdue equipment by borrower and date quickly, avoiding manual searches and helping staff recover items before another booking.',[(1,'Concurrency/shared-access benefit.'),(1,'Explains coordinated updates/consistent availability.'),(1,'Concrete scheduling consequence.'),(1,'Efficient filtered retrieval benefit.'),(1,'Explains borrower/date query compared with manual search.'),(1,'Concrete equipment-recovery consequence.')],[5],alternatives=['Accept two other developed relational-database benefits supported by A3.1.1, with distinct consequences.'])
add('1:c',['A3.3.6'],'A transaction transfers an item from store A to store B by decreasing A’s count and increasing B’s count. Describe the database state before the transaction, during its updates, and after a successful COMMIT.','Before it begins, both counts satisfy the inventory rules. During the transaction, one update may have occurred while the other is pending; those partial changes must not be treated as a completed transfer. COMMIT makes both changes durable together, leaving a consistent inventory total.',[(1,'Initially consistent counts.'),(1,'Intermediate partial updates are not a completed transfer.'),(1,'Commit preserves both updates and restores the required consistent total.')],[6],status='B',adapt='Uses the explicit 2027 commit/transaction scope rather than requiring an old named transaction-state taxonomy.')
add('1:d',['A3.3.6'],'A transaction has decreased store A’s count but fails before increasing store B’s count. Describe how ROLLBACK should affect the transaction and the inventory total.','It undoes the uncommitted decrease at A. Neither half of the failed transfer remains, so the counts return to their prior consistent values and no item disappears from the inventory.',[(1,'Undoes the uncommitted decrease.'),(1,'Neither partial transaction update persists.'),(1,'Prior consistent counts/total restored.')],[6],status='B',adapt='Retains undo/recovery reasoning through explicit ROLLBACK; log/checkpoint recovery algorithms are not claimed as requirements.')
add('2:a',['A3.1.1'],'MEASUREMENT uses (DeviceID,SensorID,Timestamp) as a composite primary key. Outline why Timestamp may be needed when the same sensor measures the same device repeatedly.','DeviceID and SensorID repeat for successive measurements. Timestamp distinguishes those events so separate readings can be stored without duplicate keys.',[(1,'Device/sensor pair alone can repeat.'),(1,'Timestamp distinguishes repeated events for uniqueness.')],[7])
add('2:b',['A3.2.5'],'READING(DeviceID,SensorID,Timestamp,DeviceName,Value) has the composite key (DeviceID,SensorID,Timestamp). DeviceID alone determines DeviceName. Outline why the relation violates 2NF.','DeviceName depends on only DeviceID, a proper subset of the composite key. This partial dependency of a non-key attribute violates 2NF.',[(1,'Identifies DeviceName depending on DeviceID only.'),(1,'Explains partial dependency on part of the composite key.')],[7])
add('2:c',['A3.3.2'],'READING rows (DeviceName,SensorID,Kind) are ("Orchid","S4","heat"), ("Cedar","S2","heat"), ("Maple","S4","heat"), ("Ash","S4","light"). State the rows returned by SELECT DeviceName FROM READING WHERE Kind=\'heat\' AND SensorID=\'S4\';.','Orchid and Maple, in either order.',[(1,'Orchid.'),(1,'Maple, with no extra rows.')],[7],status='B',adapt='Retains SQL selection/result tracing; the task supplies a complete query rather than claiming a two-table construction exercise.',ms_summary='Original names: Simon Moore and Scottie Ngatai.')
