add('4:d',['A4.3.4'],'Describe how unsupervised clustering of visitor records could help a museum plan services, and give one limitation.','Use features such as visit frequency, group size and interests to form similar groups without preassigned labels. Different groups may suggest family activities or specialist tours. Results depend on selected features and may hide differences within a cluster, so staff should investigate before treating every member alike.',[(1,'Unlabelled grouping by feature similarity.'),(1,'Appropriate concrete features.'),(1,'Service-planning application.'),(1,'Relevant feature/group-generalization limitation.')],[13],status='B')
add('4:e',['A3.4.2'],'Explain why combining different branch records in a data warehouse requires consistent identifiers and formats.','Sources may use different codes or date formats for equivalent facts. Aligning them gives integrated data that can be compared and aggregated correctly; otherwise the same visitor/site may be counted separately or dates misinterpreted.',[(1,'Recognizes incompatible source representations.'),(1,'Standardization enables integrated comparison/aggregation.'),(1,'Concrete consequence of inconsistent identifiers/formats.')],[14],status='B',adapt='Retains warehouse integration reasoning; detailed ETL stages/strategies are outside the assessed requirement.')
